/ref 1061 24219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079402 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70588abd-28cf-57e8-91ad-d7a423e0a722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079402 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079403 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079403 #edb/ref 1061 24220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079403 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8acd33b3-bbd7-5f1f-ae06-547799b4f66d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079403 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079404 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079404 #edb/ref 1061 24221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079404 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3590f636-f3a1-5592-a64c-2fb248cb17db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079404 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079405 #edb/ref 1061 24222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079405 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f98b762-c792-57e5-8e73-6917892dea16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079405 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079553 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079553 #edb/ref 1061 607934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079553 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88086b0a-3e17-59ee-bc49-6709f897fb53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079553 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079617 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2 y}{\\mathrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} + 6y =0\n$$\n\nFrom the previous slide, we know this ODE has a characteristic equation of the form $a\\lambda^2 + b\\lambda + c=0.$ Here, $a=1, b=5,$ and $c=6,$ so the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots are $\\lambda_1 = -2$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3.$ Each root gives a particular solution to the ODE: $y_1=e^{-2x}$ and $y_2=e^{-3x}.$\n\nThe **general solution** is a linear combination of these particular solutions:\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079617 #edb/ref 1065 14884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079617 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe48b08c-e63b-577c-ba34-bc61bc5b2966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079617 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079670 #edb/ref 1064 "A second-order, linear, homogeneous ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients has the general form\n\n$$\na\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + b\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + cy =0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nTo find solutions, we can propose a trial solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant to be determined. Differentiating this gives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general ODE, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a({\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x})+b(\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x})+c({e}^{\\lambda x}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}(a{\\lambda }^{2}+b\\lambda +c) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{\\lambda x}$ can never be zero, the expression in the parentheses must be zero:\n\n$$\na\\lambda^2+b\\lambda + c =0 .\n$$\n\nThis is called the **characteristic equation** (or **auxiliary equation**). The roots of this quadratic equation for $\\lambda$ give us the solutions to the original differential equation.\n\nNext, we'll use the characteristic equation to solve a specific example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079670 #edb/ref 1065 4046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079670 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c8c3fdb1-a293-55b7-bb3f-a3b91cd13c06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079670 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118304 #edb/ref 1075 #ed